Gate bootstrapping is a core primitive that enables arbitrary circuit evaluation in fully homomorphic encryption (FHE), where blind rotation remains the dominant performance bottleneck. In this work, we present a sub-millisecond NTRU-based gate bootstrapping scheme that achieves state-of-the-art performance through coordinated algorithmic, software, and hardware-level optimizations. At the algorithmic level, we introduce several techniques to accelerate blind rotation while tightly controlling noise growth. First, we propose a hybrid key design that combines key unrolling with block binary keys, overcoming the limitations of using either technique alone. This design reduces the number of blind rotation iterations by a factor of four while preserving 128-bit security. To mitigate the resulting noise amplification, we introduce a mean-elimination technique during modulus switching to suppress mean-square errors caused by non-centered secret key distributions. In addition, by increasing the initial noise of the NTRU secret key and the modulus Q, we significantly enhance noise controllability throughout the bootstrapping process. We further optimize automorphism-based bootstrapping by merging automorphism-related key switching into parameterized external products, reducing the number of automorphism operations by 95% compared to prior work. At the software level, we implement our scheme on modern CPUs using AVX-512 vectorization. Our optimized implementation achieves a bootstrapping latency of 0.95 ms, providing a 4x speedup over LLW+ (TCHES 2024), while reducing key size by 28.4× over LLW+, 31.8× over TFHE, and 230× over FHEW. At the hardware level, we design a dedicated ASIC accelerator, FastHE, achieving a record-low bootstrapping latency of 36 ps. Compared with the state-of-the-art Morphling (HPCA 2024), FastHE provides a 4.4x reduction in runtime and a 5.7x improvement in area-delay product (ADP), demonstrating that high-performance FHE is practical through end-to-end algorithm-hardware co-design.